  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nChances for isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms\ncontinue as monsoonal moisture remains over the area.\nThunderstorm development this afternoon may be enhanced a little \nby a nearby mesoscale convective vortex sitting south of the area.\nOtherwise, mountain orographic lift and day time heating will\ndevelop showers and storms that will favor the mountain zones and\nparts of Hudspeth county this afternoon and early evening. Storms\nwill redevelop again Sunday afternoon with coverage beginning to\nshift more to the western parts of the local forecast area. The \nmain impacts from these storms will be brief periods of heavy rain\nthat could cause localized minor flooding, and strong winds gusts\nmixed with blowing dust in dust prone areas. \n\nA pattern change to a drier less active weather pattern will begin\nMonday as high pressure rebuilds over the state from the east. The\nstrengthening high aloft will suppress some storm development\nwhile also pushing deeper moisture back toward Arizona. This\ndrying trend will become more pronounced as the week progresses.\nWith increasing high pressure aloft, a return to 100 degree\ntemperatures in the lowlands is expected much of next week.\nCurrent forecast models are underplaying the temperatures a few\ndegrees, warmer values expected, especially in the middle and\nlatter parts of next week. Thunderstorm development will\ncontinue occur but will be restricted to mainly the mountain\nareas where orographic lift can aid in storm development. \n\nRain chances may improve toward the end of next week as the center\nof the high pressure drifts more to the north, allowing recycled\nmoisture to move into the region for a slight improvement\nthunderstorm development.",
